   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = x^{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Power",
            "x",
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Then \\( du = 2 x \\, dx \\), so \\( x \\, dx = \\frac{1}{2} \\, du \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Since \\( F(2) = F(0) + \\int_{0}^{2} x e^{x^{2}} \\, dx \\), convert the limits: \\( x = 0 \\) gives \\( u = 0 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\) gives \\( u = 4 \\), so the integral becomes \\( \\int_{0}^{4} \\frac{e^{u}}{2} \\, du \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Exp",
               "u"
            ],
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "An antiderivative of \\( \\frac{e^{u}}{2} \\) is \\( \\frac{e^{u}}{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Exp",
               "u"
            ],
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 5,
         "text": "The integral is \\( \\frac{e^{4}}{2} - \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = - \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{e^{4}}{2} \\), so \\( F(2) = 3 + \\left(- \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{e^{4}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{5}{2} + \\frac{e^{4}}{2} \\).",
